 got into computation  and in in that time it was really  bioinformatics was very much of a niche  landscape where  there were a handful of these  specialized researchers but  nowadays nearly every discipline within  biomedical research has the needs for  bioinformatics methods  and we've really  narrowing this chasm between biology and  the computing sciences  and i i try to stand sort of in between  the two so that i can  interact with people on both sides  of these fields  so  the challenges that that i have run into  is that  as high throughput sequencing has come  onto the scene you know starting in 2005  through about 2008 getting established  and then in the last decade has really  exploded in the in the amount of  different things that you can use it for  because the cost of sequencing come down  so much  there's a plethora of sequencing data  that  needs to be analyzed  and it can't really be served anymore by  these niche  researchers in bioinformatics and  computational biology  so i've found that there's a need for  training biomedical researchers  particularly  to empower them to be able to perform  computational data analysis on their own  data  and those researchers really don't need  to be bioinformatics specialists anymore  they can actually be biomedical  researchers that learn enough to do  analysis of their own data  the problem that arises is really that  these researchers come from a diverse  set of backgrounds some of them have had  no computer programming experience  whatsoever  whereas others have been programming as  a hobby  you know for quite a bit of time  and so the traditional format or  educational programs where you you take  three people through step by step  can end up being  in a course setting  too fast for some of the people who  haven't had  this you know experience but can also be  incredibly slow for people who have had  that experience and so when you get one  of these courses  and you have these students with all  these varying backgrounds it's very  challenging to  to do a traditional lecture course  so the emerging solution that we've come  on to and and have partnered with pine  biotech in the louisiana biomedical  research network  is uh more of a reliance on technology  to provide online learning opportunities  which will allow students to move  individually at their own pace if they  can move really quickly through the  modules then they do them fast  and if they need to take some more time  they can take a bit more time and this  is really something that you can't  really accomplish in a traditional  in-person lecture format but with online  course modules  it allows people to move at their own  pace  and so  about four or five years ago i developed  uh these lecture series that i conducted  and i i conducted them in person at lsu  health sciences new orleans but we  recorded all of the lectures  using this media site capture technology  and we broadcast them also live  streaming for remote viewing for people  at lsu and other places throughout the  uh lbrn network  and then there was participation via  group forums for questions and  announcements and distribution of course  material and then we did some quizzes  and tests to  test the understanding of the material  and the idea behind this was always that  these these recorded lecture series  would persist and be available for  viewing on demand and so the two lecture  series that i gave in 2016 and 2017 are  still available  on my metagenomics search server at  one of them was a general introduction  to informatics  and the other was an introduction to  microbial community analysis and and  these are all available as slide sets  videos  on all the other lecture material is  available in archive  for download at those above urls  and interestingly uh you know some  unintended consequences occurred and i  got an email out of the blue  [Music]  from the northern new england clinical  and translational research network and i  guess they had done some searching and  they had happened upon  my lecture series and so they asked me  for permission  to link uh the lecture series for their  own investigators instead of you know  reinventing the wheel and so i think  this is you know one of the very  powerful aspects of of the online  learning paradigm is that you can reuse  these materials and and don't have to  continually reinvent the wheel and give  lecture after lecture on the same course  material  and  when we held these lecture series we  also throughout the lbrn network  provided them to our primarily  undergraduate institutes for usage and  um  they could  take the lectures that were recorded  online  and then they could add their own uh  sort of lan yap to them and so this was  done at both lsu shreveport in northern  louisiana and also at southern  university in baton rouge and they  enrolled students in courses at their  own university  and a lot of them were just topical  courses  and then they they allowed the students  to use the remote lecture material but  then the the instructor who was the  instructor of record at each individual  institution  would  partner and would provide in-person  lectures to supplement what the students  were learning  based on their needs  and so  that was done at those two institutions  and then since then the albertan has  partnered  with pine biotech and pine biotech  offers a whole library of courses that  are available online through their edu  platform  and  those are courses that students again  enroll in they take at their own pace  but then they're also if you're enrolled  in the alburn programs  are supplemented with some in-person  meetings that are done online as well  so this address is part of the critical  need for education in in the field of  bioinformatics  and so this pine biotech program it was  initially established in in the summer  of 2018 in that partnership  with pine biotech and the talbot  bioinformatics institute we had obtained  25 educational licenses as part of the  lbrn and we provided them to each of our  uh primarily undergraduate institution  campuses and they were able to enroll in  the fine biotech educational modules and  take them and so that pilot program went  so well  that um it's been expanded into a  full-blown partnership with biotech and  the students that take these uh these  modules on  genomics metagenomics transcriptomics  and and  other  topics they're encouraged to present  their their project posters at the lbr  and annual meeting and other uh other  meetings that are held a lot of them  virtually now due to the covet  restrictions  so that's been a large partnership that  has allowed the um you know  kind of expansion of the ability to to  teach bioinformatics  to  individuals who have very diverse  backgrounds and they can go at their own  pace through these course modules so  this is sort of been a whole evolution  over the past five or six years  and here's just a a snippet of the some  of the current offerings from those pine  biotech modules on transcripts  transcriptomics genomics metagenomics  and epigenomics and this is all opera  poe to the olmecs forum that we're that  we're going through right now  so one of the other things that i'll  draw your attention to is that as part  of the lbrn  and the  bioinformatics biostatistics and  computational biology core  that i direct we hold an annual  conference on bioinformatics and  computational biology every year  uh and we just held our eighth annual uh  conference the actual eighth annual  conference was originally scheduled for  april of 2020  but due to the emerging uh coveted 19  pandemic we had we didn't have time to  pivot and we actually had to cancel it  so we  we held our eighth annual conference  virtually uh april 15th to 17th  of this year  and we have  the uh recordings from all of the past  talks are available online through the  lbrm portal  on youtube  and the links are available at the uh at  the lbrn conference on  uh biology and bioinformatics  link above  and part of the the meeting that we held  just this year was a session that was  entirely organized was a whole day  organized by  todd reilly at the nih  center for information technology  and this was discussing the nih's  strides initiative which is a cloud  computing initiative  whereby they've partnered with amazon  web services research and google cloud  research and they provide training they  provide  cloud credits for researchers at embry  institutions  and we've been participating in a lot of  these trainings over the course the last  couple of months since they've been  established  and then we also had other imminent  bioinformatics and computational biology  speakers who covered topics across the  gamut of computational biology  including pandemic modeling  of covid19  the gut microbiome herpes simplex virus  and the role of statistics and  epigenetics research so this is just a  snapshot of all of our our primary  speakers  that we held at the 2021 uh conference  and the 2022 conference will be held  similarly will be held in april of 2022  so  keep an eye out that for that if you're  interested um  we're not sure quite yet whether it will  be a virtual format  whether it will be back to an in-person  format or some some form of hybrid  format so we'll see how that goes  so zooming in a little bit um that's a  lot of the initiatives that i'm evolving  involved with on a broader  scale  my own research lab at lsu health  sciences  when i built it back in 2012 i built it  with this open lab layout in order to  promote collaboration so we don't have  you know cubicles or partitions or  anything and i mean that was a great  idea back in 2012 maybe it's not such a  good idea nowadays  but the idea was that you know if  somebody's sitting there working on a  problem and they have an issue they  could turn to the person next to them  and see if they had some advice and so i  always have a mix of different  researchers in the lab at different  career stages i've had post-docs i've  had  graduate students i've had  undergraduates through our summer  research program i've even had high  school students through our summer  research program  and even a few uh junior faculty members  who wanted to come in  and learn how to analyze their own  biomedical research data  and so you know we work with them and  it's sort of this uh the old proverb of  if you give a man a fish you can eat for  a day but if you teach a man to fish you  can eat for a lifetime  so the idea is we teach these  researchers how to analyze their data  and then they they can kind of spread  their wings and and and do it on their  own in the future  and so i primarily in my own research i  work primarily with md phd students who  go through both our medical program and  our phd program and it's a joint program  and we just graduated vince maffie he  was an f30 fellow  working in alcohol research looking at  the gut microbiota  and i currently have in my lab  jacob elnager who's just submitted an  f30 grant which we hope will get funded  and he's working on  shotgun metagenomics analysis of the  vaginal microbiome with specific  emphasis on bacterial vaginosis  and then i've just hosted for a rotation  a student john lammons who did a  rotation this summer  and likely will join my lab as another  md phd student we've had several past  members of the lab as i said many  undergraduates several phd students who  work with other investigators have come  in and i've helped to co-mentor them if  they have a project in their home lab  that involves dna sequencing analysis  and so in terms of ongoing research  projects  my research really focuses in heavily  now on the vaginal microbiota and  various sexually transmitted infections  so i have a multi-pi uh  r01 grant that is studying the clearance  of chlamydia  prior to treatment so we have some women  who come in they get tested for  chlamydia they end up positive by the  time we call them back in for treatment  they've actually cleared the chlamydial  infection  through natural host defense and we're  looking at the  you know properties of the microbiome  that potentially promote  that natural clearance  prior to antibiotic treatment we've also  got a  a new ro1 that's based in south africa  and is joined with the university of  cape town and the university of southern  california  and that's looking at  adverse birth and newborn outcomes and  the interaction of the vaginal  microbiome with  preterm birth and adverse birth outcomes  then there's a an r01 that i have um  with a collaborator at uab christina  mosney to study bacterial vaginosis  and my student jacob the f30 that he  submitted is really working uh jointly  under that grant  i'm also a member of the center for  clinical and translational research at  uab  uh lsu health sciences is one of the sub  sites for that so that's this ul one  grant and then we're doing some work on  the gut microbiota and precision  medicine under a uh-2 grant  and we just got a an r21 got a fundable  score last month that this is going to  be kind of an interesting one we're  going to look at transgender men  who are just initiating testosterone use  and we're going to look at how that  impacts  the vaginal and reproductive tract  microbiota and the  propensity for getting bacterial  vaginosis after testosterone use  and then  as i mentioned i'm also part of the  louisiana biomedical research network  and more recently we've gotten an nsf  grant funded for a high performance  computing cluster so we're we're just  into the start of the second year for  that  and that grant has supported the  acquisition of a a  large computing cluster which we call  tiger fish  and it's one of the fir it's actually  the first high performance computing  that has been broadly available for our  health sciences center campus in new  orleans we've had access to  some of the clusters in um in baton  rouge and through the loaning network  but  this is for  new orleans specifically and it provides  up to 193 teraflops of compute  1400 total compute cores  it's primarily composed of these compute  nodes each of which has 192 gigabytes of  ram  but it also has available a large memory  node which has up to 1.5 terabytes of  ram available  for tasks that really need a lot of uh  of memory to perform their their compute  and so we just rolled this out we got  this cluster in april of 2021 and we're  you know we've got a bunch of users  using it for all kinds of things my  lab's using it cendo is a co-pi on this  grant his lab is using it  also carmen canaver is using it for some  computational neurobiology  and i'm you know in the interest of time  i'm not going to say much about this  because chennai covered the masters  program in bioinformatics that's been  initially it is being initiated to lsu  health sciences  and you've already got the link  to that bioinformatics program  and lastly i'll say that i'm one of the  founding members of the microbial  genomics resource group at lsu health  sciences and so you know if you follow  that link  um this is it's essentially it operates  sort of like a core but  we're not really a core so we just call  us ourselves a resource group  and it consists of myself in charge of  the bioinformatics david welsh who's a  pulmonary  medical doctor  and hmong  who does all of our sequencing prep and  sequencing and then a variety of  post-docs and students who work with us  but we have um on this website you know  we have a lot of our joint publications  and everything but down near the bottom  and also accessible via this link  is uh you know our cost and our sample  submission so we do a lot of microbial  genomics because we have an aluminum i  seek and so you know we do a lot of  microbial community analysis and we're  just starting to get into some shotgun  metagenomics as well  and so that's pretty much all i have to  say and you know if there's any time for  questions i'm happy to answer any  questions  all right great thank you

