 in baton  rouge and through the loaning network  but  this is for  new orleans specifically and it provides  up to 193 teraflops of compute  1400 total compute cores  it's primarily composed of these compute  nodes each of which has 192 gigabytes of  ram  but it also has available a large memory  node which has up to 1.5 terabytes of  ram available  for tasks that really need a lot of uh  of memory to perform their their compute  and so we just rolled this out we got  this cluster in april of 2021 and we're  you know we've got a bunch of users  using it for all kinds of things my  lab's using it cendo is a co-pi on this  grant his lab is using it  also carmen canaver is using it for some  computational neurobiology  and i'm you know in the interest of time  i'm not going to say much about this  because chennai covered the masters  program in bioinformatics that's been  initially it is being initiated to lsu  health sciences  and you've already got the link  to that bioinformatics program  and lastly i'll say that i'm one of the  founding members of the microbial  genomics resource group at lsu health  sciences and so you know if you follow  that link  um this is it's essentially it operates  sort of like a core but  we're not really a core so we just call  us ourselves a resource group  and it consists of myself in charge of  the bioinformatics david welsh who's a  pulmonary  medical doctor  and hmong  who does all of our sequencing prep and  sequencing and then a variety of  post-docs and students who work with us  but we have um on this website you know  we have a lot of our joint publications  and everything but down near the bottom  and also accessible via this link  is uh you know our cost and our sample  submission so we do a lot of microbial  genomics because we have an aluminum i  seek and so you know we do a lot of  microbial community analysis and we're  just starting to get into some shotgun  metagenomics as well  and so that's pretty much all i have to 